            0.116,
            0.143,
            0.218,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": "**Section 9.1**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.17,
            0.236,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "**Page No. 219**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.195,
            0.147,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.196,
            0.268,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "**Figure it Out**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.227,
            0.882,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "**Q1. Do you see any line of symmetry in the figures at the start of the chapter? What about in the picture of the cloud?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.271,
            0.881,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "Ans. Yes, there are 6, 4 and 1 lines of symmetry in the figures of flower, rangoli and butterfly respectively. There is no line of symmetry in the figures of pinwheel and cloud."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.316,
            0.809,
            0.335
          ],
          "content": "**Q2. For each of the following figures, identify the line(s) of symmetry if it exists.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.345,
            0.162,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": "**Ans.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.507,
            0.216,
            0.524
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Section 9.1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.534,
            0.232,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": "**Page no. 221**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.561,
            0.881,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "**Q. Is there any other way to fold the square so that the two halves overlap? How many lines of symmetry does the square shape have?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.607,
            0.521,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "Ans. No, there is no other way to fold the square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.634,
            0.502,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "The square shape has 4 lines of symmetry."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.661,
            0.881,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "**Q. We saw that the diagonal of a square is also a line of symmetry. Let us take a rectangle that is not a square. Is its diagonal a line of symmetry?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.706,
            0.582,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "Ans. No, Rectangle’s diagonal is not a line of symmetry."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.76,
            0.233,
            0.778
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Page no. 222"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.788,
            0.881,
            0.824
          ],
          "content": "**Q. What if we reflect along the diagonal from A to C? Where do points A, B, C and D go? What if we reflect along the horizontal line of symmetry?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.833,
            0.881,
            0.868
          ],
          "content": "**Ans.** If we reflect along the diagonal from A to C, D occupies the position occupied by B earlier. A and C remain at the same place."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.877,
            0.882,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": "If we reflect along the horizontal line of symmetry, D and C occupies the position earlier occupied by A and B respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.488,
            0.927,
            0.512,
            0.942
          ],
          "content": "[1]"
        }
      ]
    }
